ats  Northern Ireland differently to other  parts of the UK and that's a fundamental  problem there isn't it so what would  labor do about it well we've said from  the outset that we would fix the  problems with the protocol yeah it's  been almost 15 months since the British  government put forward its proposals for  a fast track across the uh the Irish sea  the EU it's been a year this week since  the EU before its proposals for a Green  Lane we are arguing about the same thing  it is inconceivable to me that we've not  made the progress that we need now a  year ago the dup were not saying they  were in a Stormont they were raising  concerns because of power sharing they  had the right to be taken on their own  terms and they were ignored it's only  when they actually acted if you forgive  the phrase for them in a dysfunctional  Way by pulling down the assembly and the  executive that suddenly government  started taking notice of them and in the  period since then their expectations  have grown because of the the sheer  disruption that's happened in Northern  Ireland we have allowed through Tory  neglect a dysfunction to embed itself in  Northern Ireland the divisions between  different communities to become  entrenched to become more divided than  they were before and the process that we  need to move people back together again  and heal some of these challenges and  move forward again seems just simply not  to be in place at the moment would you  say that and yet from you it feels like  there's more of the brexit blame game  going on by blaming the government now I  mean isn't it for everybody to coming  together including labor to be  absolutely right but I'm going to call  it out the way I see it I was over there  on Monday I was over there on Monday in  Stormont I met all political leaders of  all parties represented in Stormont  where was the Secretary of State he was  campaigning for Boris Johnson here in  Westminster you know we need to have  Focus now I would love to be able to get  involved in some of these things but I'm  doing everything I can do to make sure  that we we sow the seeds of  reconciliation and progress in Northern  Ireland but we need that position of  power to be able to do it we did it in  1998 from a position of Power with the  Good Friday agreement the government at  the moment are sowing divisions even  further I was over in the island of  Ireland for three days with Kia starma  we met the Irish president the Irish  president the Irish Prime Minister the  tea shop we went to the Northern Ireland  and met all the political leaders there  in Stormont we have a prime minister in  Waiting in kirstama a secretary of state  in uh waiting in me who wants to commit  the time and are already doing so from a  position of opposition we just need to  get into government so we can put it  into action okay and as far as the  elections are concerned they're now  going to be called again before  Christmas will that move things forward  do you think I think it's unlikely to  move forward but it will present another  six six months of opportunity let's make  this a six months where we don't waste  that opportunity I mean parties in  Northern Ireland I wish all parties were  there serving in storm on but it means  we also need here in Westminster to up  our game we need a Tory government that  will pay attention and create and  rebuild relationships with the Irish  government provide the framework for  peace and Reconciliation and progress in  Northern Ireland okay I want to move on  to other issues because there's so much  news around and not least the  announcement that Rishi sunak is not  going to cop27 what's your response to  that I think this is a huge shame we we  know from yesterday that the world is  not likely to meet the uh the The Net  Zero targets that we need and the the  global warming targets we need this this  would be a wake-up call for all of us we  need to be shocked into action but this  government seems to be shocked into  withdrawal you know this government has  a policy of global Britain so it's not  just about climate change even though  that is of Paramount importance it's  about Britain participating in the way  that the globe comes together the world  comes together to solve problems if  we're not there around the table on  climate change then why would the rest  of the world look to Britain to  leadership in other areas as well well  Terry's Coffee spoke to me earlier  environment Secretary of course and she  says it's not normal for all the leaders  to go every year it's normally the big  focus is on every five years and last  year cop26 was that big focus and it's  true isn't it that Richie sunac has a  huge amount on his plate he's got this  very important awesome budget which  falls in the dates of of cop27 and and  isn't it right that that should be his  focus at a time like that Teresa coffee  is wrong for many reasons let me give  you two firstly we are president of cop  right now and we're handing that over to  Eastern cop 27 we are president for a  half of the uh half of the conference  and we will hand over but we have that  moment where we're still president we're  the people that should be providing  energy commitment in into cop27 so that  we hand over a presidency that is in  good shape and we're not even turning up  as prime minister we're Banning the king  from going we're not going to have the  representation there that shows Britain  is fully committed and for the second  reason we now know that we are in a  worse State when it comes to climate  change than we were a year ago this is  more important we can't wait for five  years five yearly intervals before the  Tory prime minister gives a damn about  climate change well we now need to make  sure that we are there when those  crucial moments are being decisions are  being taken and right now we need to  make sure our presidency ends with a  bang it ends with energy going into the  next presidency because the world is  failing in its climate change emissions  and Britain's okay leading not  withdrawing he says he is committed to  to Net Zero commitments and he also is  saying that he's got lots of other  pressing concerns and not least this  Autumn statement and it is an issue the  economy isn't it I mean let me just show  you a poll that's our you may well have  seen it I think it's on the front of the  eye newspaper they've used bgm to do a  survey and they're now saying that the  new prime minister Rishi sunak is more  trusted with the economy also more  trusted with tax as and also with  business as well  compared to to case Pharma should labor  be worried about that well she said  that's only been in the job a couple of  days well he's in the in job a couple of  days which means you're usually get a  bit of a honeymoon period but the key  thing is that when kirst armor took over  as leader of the labor party he was  minus 32 percent when it came to economy  and tax and the issues you're raising  here right now he's been ahead for quite  a while considerable amount of time on  the economy and trustworth and being  trustworthy he's still seen as more  Prime ministerial than Rishi sunak but  on those key issues the thing that's  given Wishy sunak a bit of a bump and  the Tory is a bit of a bump on the  economy is because they keep adopting  labor policies on the windfall tax for  example a labor policy right now you're  going to see a whole bunch of other  areas  the fact that he was Chancellor and  oversaw the furlough and people people  seem to think that he makes sense and  the economy according to these polls at  least and they're asked the questions  over what labor would do but you see  like says he's going to miss cop he's  going to focus on on the Autumn  statement but there are questions about  how labor would fill that the Autumn  statement is intensified there's no  question about that now because we've  been talking about this since the summer  we wouldn't have had the mini budget  which crashed the economy a crisis made  in Downing Street being paid for by  every worker up and down our country so  they got us into this mess the the  Autumn statement is about how they get  us out of the mess that they got us into  so yes I think the country is relieved  that we have a new prime minister and  it's not Liz trust anymore but don't  forget that this is a government which  is made up of people through grubby  deals it's not up there it's not made up  of a government of Talon which is there  to face the challenges that the  government that the country faces right  now this is a government full of fixes  that can fix the challenges of the Tory  back benches is not a government that's  there to fix the challenges our country  cases in kirstama they they know that  they have somebody who is focused on the  priorities of the country country first  party second he said at our labor party  conference and got Innovation for it  with this lot it is party first and  Country second okay well we'll put you  down on details of the government of  Labor's tax and spending plans next time  Peter Carlo we're out of time now thanks  very much indeed

